@page "/button"

<CatHead Title="Buttons">
    <code>.btn</code> is the base; every variant is one extra class. A panel's primary call to
    action is always a <strong>filled</strong> button in its semantic colour.
</CatHead>

<p class="cat-lede">Choose a variant by meaning, not appearance.</p>
<div class="card">
    <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>.btn--brand.btn--solid</code></td><td>The neutral primary action</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.btn--go.btn--solid</code></td><td>Sends something outward — approve, apply, send, reroute</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.btn--warn.btn--solid</code></td><td>Changes who is in control — accept and take over</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.btn--warn</code></td><td>The same action as a secondary, beside a green primary</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.btn--danger</code></td><td>Destructive</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.ToneMatrix" Title="Tone and emphasis" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A tone says what a button does — <code>.btn--brand</code>, <code>--go</code>, <code>--warn</code>,
        <code>--danger</code>, <code>--info</code>, <code>--secret</code> — and an emphasis how loudly:
        soft by default, <code>.btn--solid</code> filled, <code>.btn--ghost</code> with no fill until
        hovered. Any tone takes any emphasis; a panel's one primary action is the filled one.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Variants" Title="Variants" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The variants in the rows they belong in, each at the same height: one filled button per row,
        the primary last, and a destructive action soft and on the far side with
        <code>.sedna-push</code> after it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.btn--sm</code>, the default, and <code>.btn--lg</code>. Every control that can share a row
        takes the same three heights &mdash; 28px, 36px and 44px &mdash; so a button, a link, an
        icon button, a field and a chip line up; a <a href="/badge">badge</a> does not, because it
        has its own type scale.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Pending" Title="While its action runs">
    <p>
        <code>aria-busy="true"</code> while a save or a call is in flight. The label stays in the layout
        and is painted out, so the button keeps its measured width and does not shrink under the
        cursor; a ring in the variant's own ink spins in its place, and a second pointer click is
        ignored. Leave <code>disabled</code> off — it drops focus and stops the state being
        announced — and have the handler ignore a second <kbd>Enter</kbd> while the work runs.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.IconOnly" Title="Icon-only">
    <p>
        <strong><code>.btn--icon</code> is not optional</strong> &mdash; a bare <code>.btn</code>
        keeps its text padding, so one glyph sits in a box half again as wide as it is tall. Give
        every icon-only button an <code>aria-label</code>; the glyph itself is a bundled
        <a href="https://remixicon.com">Remix Icon</a>, linked from
        <code>_content/Sedna.UI/lib/remixicon/remixicon.css</code> as
        <code>ri-*</code> on an <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Ghost" Title="Ghost">
    <p>
        <code>.btn--ghost</code> defers its border and fill until hover, for a dense row of
        secondary actions &mdash; a table row's actions, a toolbar. It keeps the same box as a
        bordered button, so a ghost and a solid button in one row still line up.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Bare" Title="In a line of text" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.btn-bare</code> is a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with no background, border or
        padding, taking its font and colour from the text around it — for an identifier that
        <em>acts</em>: a record number that opens the record, a key with a copy button beside it, a
        chevron that unfolds a section. <code>.btn-bare--link</code> colours it like a link, for one
        that goes somewhere.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Write it without <code>.btn</code></strong> — it is the absence of the button's
        chrome, not a variant of it, and both classes together bring the height and the hover fill
        back. It keeps the library's focus ring, which is the declaration a hand-written inline reset
        forgets: removing the border removes the browser's own outline with it. Tab through this
        example to see it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.NameThatIsReadBut" Title="A name that is read but not shown">
    <p>
        <code>.visually-hidden</code> takes text out of the rendering and leaves it in the
        accessibility tree &mdash; here the button shows a count and the hidden span says what the
        count is of. Do not reach for <code>display:none</code> or a zero-size box instead: both
        drop the element from the accessibility tree too.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Link" Title="As a link">
    <p>
        <code>.btn</code> works on an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> &mdash; it sets its own
        <code>text-decoration</code> and colour, so no reset is needed.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Wrap" Title="A label on two lines">
    <p>
        <code>.btn--wrap</code> lets the label wrap and starts it at the inline start, for a
        picker whose choices are full-width buttons with a long name. A button is otherwise one line.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.Fill" Title="As wide as its container" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.btn--fill</code> makes a lone action as wide as its container &mdash; one large touch
        target under a list on a phone. It keeps its size tier's height, and a label too long for the
        width wraps. <code>.tabs--fill</code> and <code>.segmented--fill</code> are the same modifier
        for a row of options.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.DisabledWithHint" Title="A disabled button that says why">
    <p>
        A disabled button still takes the pointer, so a <code>data-tip</code> on it shows on hover and
        says why it cannot be used. A <code>disabled</code> button is never focused, so a keyboard
        reader cannot reach its hint; where the reason matters to them, use
        <code>aria-disabled="true"</code>, which stays in the tab order and looks the same &mdash; and
        have the handler ignore it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.FooterRow" Title="In a footer row" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <a href="/card"><code>.card-foot</code></a> is end-aligned: Cancel before the primary,
        primary last &mdash; the reading order ends on the action.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.FloatingActionButton" Title="Floating action button" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.fab</code> is one page-level action, pinned to a corner on a screen too small to show
        a toolbar; <code>.fab--icon</code> is the circular,
        label-in-an-<code>aria-label</code> form. Shown here <code>position:static</code>; in an app
        it is fixed to the corner and must stay below every overlay.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Button.SpeedDial" Title="Speed dial" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open it.</strong> <code>.fab-dial</code> on a <code>.menu-anchor</code>
        takes the FAB's corner and fans a <code>.menu</code> of a few related actions out above the
        button, each a pill on the button's end edge; the icon turns while it is open.
        <code>data-menu-toggle</code> on the <code>.fab</code> gives it a menu's behaviour &mdash;
        <code>aria-expanded</code>, <kbd>Escape</kbd> back to the button, the arrow keys between the
        actions, a press outside closing it. Three to five actions; past that it is a
        <a href="/menu">menu</a> or a <a href="/drawer">sheet of actions</a>. Shown here
        <code>position:static</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>
